Ladybird resolves an intrinsic max-height on a box with a preferred
aspect ratio to the box’s transferred size, which then caps the box’s
automatic minimum height. Blink does the same: In its
ComputeBlockSizeForFragmentInternal(), BlockSizeFunc returns
BlockSizeFromAspectRatio() for SizeType::kContent, and
ResolveMaxBlockLength() resolves the keyword through it. Then
ComputeMinMaxBlockSizes() clamps the automatic minimum by the result.
Gecko differs: nsLayoutUtils::IsAutoBSize() treats an intrinsic
keyword as the initial value in the block axis, so the content minimum
isn’t capped. And WebKit’s RenderBox::constrainLogicalHeightByMinMax()
resolves the keyword through computeLogicalHeightUsing() with the
intrinsic content height. Chrome and Ladybird make a 200px-wide square
box holding 300px of content 200px tall, and Firefox 300px tall.
